# 17. Install Flannel — Including the Multi-NIC Vagrant Fix

This is the other major networking fix.

Our Vagrant machines have two interfaces:

```text
eth0 = 10.0.2.x
eth1 = 192.168.56.x
```

If Flannel automatically chooses the NAT interface, Kubernetes pod networking can fail.

Therefore we explicitly configure Flannel to use:

```text
eth1
```

First download the standard Flannel manifest:

```bash
curl -L \
  https://github.com/flannel-io/flannel/releases/latest/download/kube-flannel.yml \
  -o kube-flannel.yml
```

Verify that the file exists:

```bash
ls -l kube-flannel.yml
```

Now locate the Flannel daemon arguments:

```bash
grep -n "kube-flannel" kube-flannel.yml
```

and:

```bash
grep -n "\-\-ip-masq" kube-flannel.yml
```

The Flannel container arguments normally contain something similar to:

```yaml
args:
  - --ip-masq
  - --kube-subnet-mgr
```

We want:

```yaml
args:
  - --ip-masq
  - --kube-subnet-mgr
  - --iface=eth1
```

Add the interface automatically:

```bash
sed -i.bak \
  '/- --kube-subnet-mgr/a\
        - --iface=eth1' \
  kube-flannel.yml
```

Verify the change:

```bash
grep -A5 \
  "\-\-kube-subnet-mgr" \
  kube-flannel.yml
```

You should see:

```text
- --kube-subnet-mgr
- --iface=eth1
```

Now apply Flannel:

```bash
kubectl apply -f kube-flannel.yml
```

Watch startup:

```bash
kubectl get pods \
  -n kube-flannel \
  -o wide \
  -w
```

Once they are running, press:

```text
Ctrl+C
```

Verify:

```bash
kubectl get pods \
  -n kube-flannel \
  -o wide
```

There should currently be one Flannel pod on the control plane.

After workers join, there should be one on every Kubernetes node.

